{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792829421,"nonce":"Gx0KwrUJlYmNBaTEtFw+lk4Pqv0=","title":"Python: SOR re-reads the grid's fields on every access because a float store may alias them","message":"scimark SOR (CI: 1.65x behind PyPy on Linux, 1.07x on macOS) runs one 5000-cycle iteration: on the NUC, LLVM tier 365 to 418 ms, Cranelift alone 450 to 462 ms, PyPy 207 to 216 ms. Its optimised loop allocates nothing on the hot path (the mallocs in SOR_execute are IndexError raises), but every G[x, y] reads G.width, G.height, G.data and the data list's header again: the loop's one store writes an element through the buffer pointer read from the header, a loaded pointer whose root LICM cannot tell apart from G (a parameter of exact type Array2D).\n\nHand-hoisting the field reads shows what is at stake (NUC, LLVM tier, 100x100, 5000 cycles, /tmp/zab/sorh.py): hoisted 0.18 to 0.21 s against PyPy 0.27 s. A list's element buffer is a separate allocation, made and reallocated only by the list's own operations, so a pointer read from offset 0 of a list header never points into an object or a header; MemLoc could carry that as its own root kind.","files":null}]}